  "Built as the McClean Public Library with $20,000 from Carnegie (in 1913, says Waymarking; Wikipedia's list says 1903) and a bequest of $21,343.42 left by Louisa McClean in 1910; it opened in October (the city museum) or on November 11 (Waymarking), 1914. Waymarking names Thomas McLaughlin of Lima as architect, the museum McLaughlin and Hulskin. Additions of 1968, when it took Arthur Kaubisch's name, and 1989 have all but hidden it: one first outside wall still shows on the south side.",
